 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Attribute Quantization](1912-attribute-quantization.md)
-specializes in reducing the precision of voxel attributes (colors and normals) using perceptually uniform spaces (CIELAB) and geometric mappings (Octahedron Normal Vectors) to maintain visual quality with fewer bits.
+Attribute quantization reduces the precision of voxel attributes (colors and normals) to lower memory footprint while maintaining perceptual quality [1912-attribute-quantization.md]. It differs from [Scalar Quantization](5726-scalar-quantization.md) and [Uniform Quantization](5855-uniform-quantization.md) by specializing the quantization method based on data type, using perceptually uniform color spaces (CIELAB) and geometric mappings (Octahedron Normal Vectors) rather than generic linear mappings [1912-attribute-quantization.md].
 
 ### [Scalar Quantization](5726-scalar-quantization.md)
-computes range parameters locally per block to enable parallel GPU decompression, mapping voxel values to a local range defined by block-specific minimum and maximum values.
+Scalar quantization (per-block) is a random-access decompression method that reconstructs voxel values on-the-fly from compressed data [5726-scalar-quantization.md]. It solves the problem of storing high-precision volume data by mapping a block of voxels to a local range defined by block-specific minimum and maximum values, allowing for parallel GPU decompression without loading full-resolution data into memory [5726-scalar-quantization.md]. It differs from general scalar quantization by computing range parameters locally per block rather than globally, and from hardware-accelerated block-coding by relying on software-defined linear interpolation formulas [5726-scalar-quantization.md].
 
 ### [Uniform Quantization](5855-uniform-quantization.md)
-maps data to evenly spaced intervals using global or local bounds, serving as the baseline method due to its simplicity, though it may fail for data with wide dynamic ranges.
+Uniform quantization is a lossy compression method that maps a continuous or high-bit-depth source domain to a discrete set of indices using evenly spaced intervals [5855-uniform-quantization.md]. It serves as the baseline for volume data compression, reducing memory footprint by representing large raw data values (e.g., 32-bit floats) with a limited number of bits (e.g., 8 bits) [5855-uniform-quantization.md]. Unlike non-uniform or adaptive quantization, it does not allocate levels based on data distribution, making it simple to implement but potentially ineffective for data with wide dynamic ranges [5855-uniform-quantization.md].
 
 ### [Chroma Subsampling](6512-chroma-subsampling.md)
-reduces color information bitrate by lowering the spatial resolution of chrominance components (U, V) while maintaining full resolution for luminance (Y), using a median-based approach to handle sparse volumetric data.
+Chroma subsampling for volumetric data reduces the bitrate of color information by lowering the spatial resolution of chrominance components (U, V) while maintaining full resolution for luminance (Y) [6512-chroma-subsampling.md]. It solves the problem of significant color information loss and "grey object" artifacts that occur when direct subsampling is applied to sparse volumetric data at low bitrates [6512-chroma-subsampling.md]. Unlike direct subsampling, it defines one color value for every eight adjacent corners using a median-based approach, preserving color quality better than naive approaches [6512-chroma-subsampling.md].
 
 ### [TetSplat Pre-processing and Data Layout](7221-tetsplat-pre-processing-and-data-layout.md)
-quantizes scalar field values and packs them into binary bitstrings for efficient storage within the TetSplat system, integrating hierarchical encoding of geometric attributes.
